WEBVTT

00:05.081 --> 00:15.949
[SPEAKER_01]: And I was like, what should I do? So I shout out a little bit, hey! And then just turned the head. When he saw me, he was just diving down like a bomb

00:16.149 --> 00:27.097
[SPEAKER_01]: and never seen again. So I think it was, this guy was scared at the moment. And I was also scared because I was so close. I was expecting that on a

00:27.117 --> 00:32.762
[SPEAKER_01]: certain stage he said, sorry guys, today I don't fly. And that was it. He never flew again.

00:43.686 --> 00:53.832
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, every now and then we come across someone in our sport who just loves being in the air so much that they dedicate their lifetime for

00:53.852 --> 01:03.577
[SPEAKER_00]: this. And today's guest is no different. I'm talking about the legendary hang gliding pilot from Austria called Manfred Rumer. More

01:03.617 --> 01:14.435
[SPEAKER_00]: than three decades of flying all kinds of wings, fixed, collapsibles, flexibles. So join us as he sits down in the third installment of Risk vs.

01:14.475 --> 01:23.300
[SPEAKER_00]: Reward as we explore the best possibilities to explore risk, the physiological and the psychological demands of flying, and how the

01:23.320 --> 01:33.365
[SPEAKER_00]: sport has evolved over all this period of time to offer both greater rewards and new challenges. So tune in, as I'm sure this episode offers

01:33.405 --> 01:37.987
[SPEAKER_00]: something for everyone who practices the art of flight in all its glory.

01:44.349 --> 01:51.314
[SPEAKER_00]: Manfred Rumer, thank you so much for joining us. It's an absolute honor to have you on Paragliding Atlas. Oh, thank you. Thank you for the

01:51.354 --> 02:02.901
[SPEAKER_01]: invitation. It's the first time that I'm in such a meeting or such a conversation. I like it already. Yeah, good. Thank you. That's so good

02:02.921 --> 02:09.666
[SPEAKER_00]: to see. And it's an absolute pleasure having you just before the Coupé Car. How does your days look like these days? I think they must be super

02:09.686 --> 02:16.402
[SPEAKER_00]: hectic with all the preparations for the Expo and everything that's coming up. Yeah, we're here in Icaro because we have a stand there with

02:16.462 --> 02:24.809
[SPEAKER_01]: the helmets. Of course, the girls, they are in charge of that business. They are quite busy. And of course, I think everything will be ready in

02:25.349 --> 02:35.236
[SPEAKER_01]: time. And for me, I will be there with the Swift again. This is my new Swift from this year to do some flight demonstration in the afternoon. I think

02:35.297 --> 02:43.365
[SPEAKER_01]: it's Saturday and Sunday in the afternoon with the hang gliding. There's like... It's like a window where we can demonstrate our

02:43.385 --> 02:52.494
[SPEAKER_01]: gliders. And some possibilities, yes. That's very cool to hear. And for the perspective of our audiences, Manfred has been working with Icaro

02:52.554 --> 03:00.239
[SPEAKER_00]: and is a hang gliding pioneer. Three decades in the out of flight with fixed wings and collapsible wings. Manfred, just a little bit of your

03:00.299 --> 03:08.123
[SPEAKER_00]: introduction for those people who are only paragliders, because since this is mostly a paragliders audience, who are you and how did you manage

03:08.163 --> 03:16.391
[SPEAKER_00]: to get, how did you manage to touch the sky with glory all this while for so long? Yeah, okay, I'm Manfred Rohmer. I'm nearly 60 years old, 59 this

03:16.431 --> 03:17.552
[SPEAKER_01]: year. Shit. Time is bad.

03:20.954 --> 03:30.042
[SPEAKER_01]: And when I was a young boy, I was interested in, I don't know, probably my mother was folding some paper planes, very basic. So I started playing

03:30.082 --> 03:41.092
[SPEAKER_01]: with that. That's how I got into aero model, model RC model, later RC model. But I was pretty much designing indoor models. They were flying

03:41.152 --> 03:52.357
[SPEAKER_01]: like real planes with profile and so on like that. But really, I got fascinated. And then later, of course, with some RC models. And then I

03:52.397 --> 04:01.261
[SPEAKER_01]: started hang gliding in 86. And from there on, it was like, that's cool. That's really good. And 86 was also the birth of paragliding. So that

04:01.321 --> 04:11.066
[SPEAKER_01]: year when I started hang gliding, there was no real paraglider. So just some years later, they came along. Because I started in Austria, upper

04:11.206 --> 04:20.869
[SPEAKER_01]: Austria, on the north side of the Alps. So it was more hilly area. not that big mountains so the first paragliders they didn't perform well in that

04:20.989 --> 04:33.973
[SPEAKER_01]: area just later let's say 88 89 90 they got there doing better and better and yeah i think i was already i think 87 i started small competition 88

04:35.273 --> 04:45.294
[SPEAKER_01]: the first austrian championship which i got through it I don't know how I did that, but I was like, oh, that's not that difficult. So in 1989, I was

04:45.394 --> 04:54.758
[SPEAKER_01]: in the first world championship in Fiersch. And from there on, every year competing, every year going to competition until 2000 when I

04:54.778 --> 05:07.244
[SPEAKER_01]: stopped with class one flag swing competitions. And then I came back again and then yes and no. But it was also 2002 when I started first flying

05:07.285 --> 05:16.620
[SPEAKER_01]: the class two gliders, the Swift, which was always fascinating to me, the technology and the possibilities. And that's how I ended up now

05:16.640 --> 05:24.845
[SPEAKER_01]: flying also the latest generation of the Swift. I think just the fact that you're nearly 60, which is, you look much younger, just to let you

05:24.886 --> 05:34.294
[SPEAKER_00]: know. And you're still demonstrating at an expo like Coupé Car. It's so inspiring to see that, Manfred. And I hope a lot of us listeners who

05:34.374 --> 05:44.120
[SPEAKER_00]: aspire to reach that stage can. But the first thing that comes to my mind is consistency. How did you do this, Manfred? Or let's say you were

05:44.180 --> 05:49.981
[SPEAKER_00]: fascinated with the art of flight when you were very young. There's something about folding paper planes that excites all of us. Your own

05:50.061 --> 05:58.962
[SPEAKER_00]: creation, when it glides. Even today, I have a bunch of paper planes sitting right on my shelf in the left-hand corner here, which I just, you

05:58.982 --> 06:07.204
[SPEAKER_00]: know, whenever I find a piece of paper, I just fold it into a plane. It's very instinctive. I got oval already about this stage here. LAUGHTER

06:08.541 --> 06:15.527
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because you're flying better than better. When my son was younger, of course, I teach them. And he was like in the kindergarten. He

06:15.567 --> 06:23.514
[SPEAKER_01]: was throwing paper planes also there. And he was like the teachers there, the big ones. They said, what this guy is doing there always.

06:23.614 --> 06:33.442
[SPEAKER_01]: After the education in free time, he was just throwing paper planes. And also he hooked up the other young kids. So that was, I think it's good to

06:33.482 --> 06:44.859
[SPEAKER_01]: put kids into the direction just with the game, with playing and having fun and also learning what's important, why these things are flying. So

06:44.899 --> 06:53.817
[SPEAKER_01]: the very basic, it's a good thing to start. Now, I agree. And I think the fascination is very infectious. And that's why this whole audience and

06:53.997 --> 07:02.602
[SPEAKER_00]: us who are chasing the art of free flight are here today. But as I was saying, starting from the beginning of your time until now, you have

07:02.642 --> 07:10.067
[SPEAKER_00]: pioneered and you have made your dream come true, which all of us manifest a lot of times. Usually, how have you, if you have to focus back

07:10.107 --> 07:18.994
[SPEAKER_00]: and look at your motivation levels or making it happen or realizing all of this, what has been the key factor in you actually succeeding to live a

07:19.014 --> 07:27.333
[SPEAKER_00]: lifestyle like this over the past, let's say, 50 years or 40 years when you actually started living life on your own terms? Yeah, I think when I

07:27.373 --> 07:35.338
[SPEAKER_01]: started hang gliding, it was just a fascination to get in the air. I think it's generally free flying, like paragliding, hang gliding, you don't

07:35.418 --> 07:46.906
[SPEAKER_01]: need much technology. Now it's really, there's also quite a lot of technology involved to make them good and safe and whatever. But with

07:46.946 --> 07:55.188
[SPEAKER_01]: little money, you put your body in the air. And that's the fascinating thing. The first flights, And then, of course, when you do some a little

07:55.208 --> 08:03.292
[SPEAKER_01]: bit longer flight soaring, starting thermaling and having that possibilities. And then especially when there are some friends

08:03.352 --> 08:12.477
[SPEAKER_01]: around, you can share that experience. And so you can push each other pushes a little, you know. And then, of course, I started competing

08:12.557 --> 08:19.718
[SPEAKER_01]: quite early. And before I started really cross-country flying, I started competing. So I learned cross-country flying in the

08:19.758 --> 08:30.360
[SPEAKER_01]: competitions. I enjoy flying cross-country by my own. But for a long time, I was just competing. You can match one to the other in pretty much

08:30.400 --> 08:38.981
[SPEAKER_01]: the same condition. And then you learn a lot. You learn big things. That's the thing which I really like in competition. But you also have to

08:39.021 --> 08:48.967
[SPEAKER_01]: be the competition guy. You have to be motivated. Because if people are going, yes, I want to compete, but I don't really want. But that's the

08:49.007 --> 08:58.573
[SPEAKER_01]: wrong thing. So you really have to. I traveled a lot in the world at the end. So I have seen many places which I never would have seen when I just

08:58.633 --> 09:07.376
[SPEAKER_01]: fly my home base. So that's the nice thing also. So yeah, that was good times, especially at the beginning. But of course, beginning is a

09:07.416 --> 09:19.422
[SPEAKER_01]: little bit, you have to learn. You have to be also a little bit careful not to over-push it, taking too much risk because your experience is not

09:19.502 --> 09:29.268
[SPEAKER_01]: that high. You think, oh, that's easy. But then you sometimes get into a situation where later you can say, yeah, I was a little bit lucky and that

09:29.328 --> 09:38.471
[SPEAKER_01]: could have been gone also quite bad. like some outlanding places or whatever, or some situations in the air, like when there are many people

09:38.531 --> 09:47.913
[SPEAKER_01]: flying together, especially at the start. For me, the collision is a big thing. So be careful. You have to learn about it. Indeed. And I think

09:48.053 --> 09:54.838
[SPEAKER_00]: you've touched upon a very important point over here that is like being careful in the beginning of your career. So let's say when you go out

09:54.898 --> 10:03.684
[SPEAKER_00]: there, the fascination of flying itself is very overwhelming for all of us. But fascination of flying with 100 other humans in a gaggle or in a

10:03.724 --> 10:11.109
[SPEAKER_00]: thermal is wow. That's a whole different feeling. You're in a whole different... If you're on top of that gaggle, it's nice. But if you're in

10:11.149 --> 10:13.671
[SPEAKER_01]: between, like in the boar, it's not so much fun.

10:17.080 --> 10:26.648
[SPEAKER_01]: It's amazing. It's really fascinating. Yeah, the view from above is magical to look at. And I think this is where we can start with the topic

10:26.748 --> 10:35.912
[SPEAKER_00]: where it's as a beginner, the fascination, the pull to be there is very strong. But it comes with a certain bit of risk. And it comes, as you

10:35.952 --> 10:41.555
[SPEAKER_00]: mentioned, you're out there competing with some of the best and some of the ones who are not so motivated, but still want to be there. So there's

10:41.895 --> 10:51.599
[SPEAKER_00]: all kinds of dynamics going around in that era. How do you usually make your decision-making when your experience is not so much? Because the

10:51.659 --> 10:58.341
[SPEAKER_00]: pull, the motivation has gotten you there somehow. You've made your resources count, you've put in the money, you've bought the equipment,

10:58.422 --> 11:06.865
[SPEAKER_00]: you've trained enough to be there. At the same time, you do not have the experience to make all the right decisions according to the best ones.

11:07.405 --> 11:17.165
[SPEAKER_00]: So how can a beginner navigate such a thing while still keeping the motivation and being relevant in the competition and in its own mind? I

11:17.225 --> 11:24.409
[SPEAKER_01]: think, as you said already, that they're trained already. It's important before joining competition that you have done already a lot

11:24.449 --> 11:33.850
[SPEAKER_01]: of flights. You're confident. Your technique has to be good. Take off, thermal flying, especially landing in small places. So that's the

11:33.930 --> 11:43.314
[SPEAKER_01]: basic thing of what is needed for competition. You have to know. Like with hang glider, the thing, the outlanding is not that easy in small

11:43.374 --> 11:53.270
[SPEAKER_01]: fields. So you have to know the technique, like with the traction, how you use it. To get really in small fields, that helps a lot. And then, of

11:53.350 --> 12:02.701
[SPEAKER_01]: course, to go to competition is to learn, to see what's going on. It's a learning process. Some people learn fast. Some people take a little bit

12:02.741 --> 12:12.037
[SPEAKER_01]: longer. But I never went to competition. And right from the beginning, I was like, One day I want to be a world champion. That was never the goal. It

12:12.077 --> 12:22.761
[SPEAKER_01]: was so far away. I was thinking, if I get to reach a regional champion, that would be great. Just the little steps by steps. Then you reach that

12:23.261 --> 12:33.385
[SPEAKER_01]: and then, then. Because I got very quick in the national team. That was also like, because I'm, let's say I, I learned quick. I learned, I was

12:33.545 --> 12:43.574
[SPEAKER_01]: motivated. The motivation is important. Of course. So that's why I stepped up quite quick to end up in the, but also like the world, the first

12:43.634 --> 12:53.457
[SPEAKER_01]: world championship, I had some good days and some bad days. And of course I was learning that time. I was learning a lot. And so later I progressed

12:53.937 --> 13:04.557
[SPEAKER_01]: better, quicker and had better constant results to end up in top position. Yeah, I think that that does sum it up a lot. But there's one big

13:04.617 --> 13:12.341
[SPEAKER_00]: dilemma that personally comes to me as well. And a lot of times I've asked this to my guests who are there on the show. It's like defining the sweet

13:12.401 --> 13:22.041
[SPEAKER_00]: spot, identifying that line between irrational fear that is holding back your progress. Taking acceptable risk where you know that, all

13:22.081 --> 13:30.004
[SPEAKER_00]: right, I can push this, but still safely. How do you try and identify that? Gut feeling is one of the answers that I get a lot of times. But

13:30.044 --> 13:40.247
[SPEAKER_00]: again, unless you push outside your comfort zone, it's very hard to define how much you can trust on your gut. So irrational fear versus

13:40.287 --> 13:47.290
[SPEAKER_00]: acceptable risk. What is your take on this after spending more than three decades in the air at the top level? Yeah, I don't know how to say

13:47.310 --> 13:58.874
[SPEAKER_01]: that. It's... The thing is, what we really have to know is we should not take risks that we get hurt and then you cannot fly the next day. So I was

13:58.974 --> 14:07.857
[SPEAKER_01]: lucky always if I had a bad landing or a little accident, I always could fly the next day. The worst was I twisted the leg, but the next day was the

14:07.897 --> 14:16.429
[SPEAKER_01]: last day of the competition. So I was jumping off the hill and that was okay. But then I had to stop a little while. That's one thing. And the

14:16.489 --> 14:25.871
[SPEAKER_01]: other thing is, of course, we fly paragliders and hang gliders, which are a great machine in the right conditions. But they have their limit.

14:26.711 --> 14:36.813
[SPEAKER_01]: And the limit is, let's say, it's the machine itself, but also the pilot. Like in paragliding, hang gliding, certain situations, you have to

14:36.893 --> 14:48.820
[SPEAKER_01]: react the right way. That's an important thing to know and to train. and how to react, especially in paragliding. They are stable, they are

14:48.901 --> 14:59.229
[SPEAKER_01]: open, they are good. I did also paragliding for two years. for a long time ago. And I was nearly jumping in the competition scene as well. I had the

14:59.269 --> 15:08.457
[SPEAKER_01]: possibility. But at the end, I was so much in hang gliding, so I did not do that step. I was thinking I should have done that, maybe yes and no. But at

15:08.477 --> 15:17.584
[SPEAKER_01]: the end, I never did it. So maybe it was a pity I could have done it. But anyway, there's a certain risk of the machine itself. And the other one

15:17.624 --> 15:26.753
[SPEAKER_01]: is the pilot. And of course, the pilot takes the decisions But sometimes it's like the course is this direction and there's a bad part,

15:26.773 --> 15:36.463
[SPEAKER_01]: especially in the Alps, let's say like this, when there are well winds and that creates more turbulence on some places where you have to go and

15:36.503 --> 15:45.269
[SPEAKER_01]: you have to climb. And sometimes it's bad when you are there in this situation. You think, holy shit, what I'm doing, really bad

15:45.289 --> 15:54.576
[SPEAKER_01]: turbulences and bad experience. But then when you go ahead, you complete the course, after landing, it's nearly gone. You say, okay,

15:54.596 --> 16:05.885
[SPEAKER_01]: that was not nice, but... No problem. So that happens from time to time. So we are quite good in just putting aside this negative experience

16:05.985 --> 16:18.337
[SPEAKER_01]: during a flight. And a lot of this is just head work. It's what's in your mind. It's really the psychological part. It really can put you down. Or

16:18.377 --> 16:31.165
[SPEAKER_01]: you just say, okay, that was... If you find a reason why it was so bad or it was nearly accident or whatever, we always, when we go in the air, we take

16:31.305 --> 16:41.332
[SPEAKER_01]: certain risk. Of course, if the air is calm and the conditions are nice, the risk is very high. The risk gets higher and higher when we fly in more

16:41.372 --> 16:50.857
[SPEAKER_01]: extreme conditions. Then there, it's for some people, it's the limit over the limit. And for some people, it's still okay. But I... During

16:50.897 --> 16:59.694
[SPEAKER_01]: competition, I was always even Over the time, we learned, like in this condition, we should not fly. So we canceled the day, even if it, like

16:59.754 --> 17:09.518
[SPEAKER_01]: now, the safety standards or the safety precaution is, yeah, maybe for half of the field, it's okay. But for the other half of the field, it's not

17:09.558 --> 17:17.742
[SPEAKER_01]: okay. So then we cancel and then we go free flying. And still, it's quite okay. So that's a good thing, not to push meteorological limits,

17:18.102 --> 17:28.664
[SPEAKER_01]: especially wind speeds in some areas can be really a problem. Yeah. Yeah, that's pretty much. I don't know. The important thing is you don't

17:28.704 --> 17:38.450
[SPEAKER_01]: have to get hurt. You don't have to hurt yourself because the ground is hard. It's not forgiving. It's bad. And there are too many or quite a lot

17:38.490 --> 17:49.773
[SPEAKER_01]: of accidents that should not happen. Probably people could have avoided, but then it's too late. Yeah. broken hand or whatever. Got it. I

17:49.813 --> 17:56.475
[SPEAKER_00]: think you've covered a lot of topics in here that I was coming to, but eventually if I have to sum it up, it's basically the flight envelope of

17:56.495 --> 18:03.837
[SPEAKER_00]: the aircraft, like what conditions can the aircraft fly in? Is it able to handle this way, these kinds of winds and gusts or whatever is being

18:03.877 --> 18:12.801
[SPEAKER_00]: forecasted and the flight envelope of the pilot, like how much trust and ability does the pilot have to handle that aircraft? Again, all of these

18:12.821 --> 18:20.069
[SPEAKER_00]: are relative depending upon people, how they look at it. But what I do eventually understand is that there are some things which are black and

18:20.109 --> 18:26.616
[SPEAKER_00]: white, such as forecasting. In competitions, there are organizers which are doing the decision-making for you. Free flight, of course,

18:26.656 --> 18:34.544
[SPEAKER_00]: you can do that with a fair bit of background research. You can make a very wise decision on forecasting and not push the limit in that one. But what

18:34.564 --> 18:45.351
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm wondering is people trying to push the limits and becoming a better version of themselves, can they possibly set up some safety protocols

18:45.831 --> 18:54.337
[SPEAKER_00]: which they can try and stick with for longevity of career, longevity of competing span and all of that? Because technology, let's say, all

18:54.357 --> 19:03.243
[SPEAKER_00]: right, you want to fly a safer wing, go ahead, buy an A glider or a mid-B glider or a two-liner, whatever your threshold is, depending upon

19:03.303 --> 19:11.345
[SPEAKER_00]: that. Equipment, like harness equipment, you want to make sure that you're not so good at landings. Instead of going in for the paper-thin,

19:11.645 --> 19:19.968
[SPEAKER_00]: super lightweight harness, you can go for something with a bit of a back protection, which takes care of you. But when it comes to designing all

19:20.008 --> 19:28.471
[SPEAKER_00]: of these things, have you, over a period of time, set some goals for you that, all right, this is my comfort zone. I'm not going to push it until I

19:28.611 --> 19:38.136
[SPEAKER_00]: actually succeed in becoming very comfortable in that one. And then I will explore outside this envelope that I've created for myself. Yeah,

19:38.216 --> 19:46.983
[SPEAKER_01]: I can. In this case, of course, I can only talk about hang gliding. I had the luck or the possibility to come to a manufacturer, EcoRoad, this

19:47.023 --> 19:59.229
[SPEAKER_01]: time for more than 30 years now. And I had the possibility to bring my own ideas and the design and to optimize the glide. And also I had to quickly

19:59.250 --> 20:09.580
[SPEAKER_01]: understand what makes a glider safe and not safe anymore. Still, let's say with the hang glider, every certified glider, let's say even the

20:09.660 --> 20:18.989
[SPEAKER_01]: safest glider, which I don't know which glider should it be. It's possible to go up, make a whip stall, push out and you can tumble

20:19.029 --> 20:31.599
[SPEAKER_01]: everywhere. even with the bad certification rules. So that's a system problem of the wing and the low CG and the CG you can move so much because

20:31.659 --> 20:40.366
[SPEAKER_01]: it's bedshift control, which is easy, nice to fly and it's fun, but there's some limits. And there's the other limit, which in the design

20:40.406 --> 20:50.349
[SPEAKER_01]: you can... you have to understand how it works but let's say a swept wing like a hang glider with that kind of profile that has a certain negative

20:50.429 --> 21:01.210
[SPEAKER_01]: momentum of the profile itself it needs then with full tide which she a minimum of twist to to compensate that let's say negative moment

21:01.311 --> 21:12.750
[SPEAKER_01]: profile sections of the wing and that's the limit so when you fly full tide with she at higher speed And you get unloaded, like usually we fly

21:12.790 --> 21:23.654
[SPEAKER_01]: with 1G and turbulence more or less. But when you get unloaded, the twist has to stay there. So the glider will not... Sometimes it happens still

21:23.714 --> 21:33.135
[SPEAKER_01]: with some gliders, they are older. The regulation is not there, not checked, or the people don't know. But I can tell, let's say, on takeoff,

21:34.176 --> 21:44.208
[SPEAKER_01]: I can look at some high-performance glider. Okay, put on your VG, and I can see by the eyes how big is the war shot on the... standing because it's

21:44.268 --> 21:52.978
[SPEAKER_01]: like a flying situation, complete weightless, let's say like this. So I can say if this glider is still safe on the safe side or not. Then

21:53.098 --> 22:02.705
[SPEAKER_01]: sometimes I tell to explain to guys, listen, in certain situations, this is not so fun. This could be dangerous. Because what happened can be

22:02.765 --> 22:14.014
[SPEAKER_01]: that you fly faster, let's say 60, 70, 80, you get unloaded, the twist goes down. It's like pushing forward on a sailplane and then it starts a

22:14.034 --> 22:22.340
[SPEAKER_01]: negative loop and you cannot do anything because you get more and more weightless. And then you make, let's say, a high speed tumbling. Oh my

22:22.360 --> 22:30.383
[SPEAKER_01]: God. This one you can avoid absolutely with the right design elements. So you can fly even in strong turbulence at higher speed and you have a

22:30.443 --> 22:38.166
[SPEAKER_01]: stable, good, safe glide. So that's the thing that is very important, let's say like this. Otherwise, because even you could fly a glider

22:38.226 --> 22:50.469
[SPEAKER_01]: without dive sticks and this tip limitation for the sail outside. But pretty much, even in bad conditions, just with one key of weight, you

22:51.009 --> 23:04.438
[SPEAKER_01]: create that natural twist and the glider still flies, still is safe. Like the old Rogalo types, which they had no of this safety things. So

23:04.458 --> 23:14.147
[SPEAKER_01]: over the years they understood the constructors, the manufacturers. But also, of course, then the certification that took care about this

23:14.187 --> 23:24.020
[SPEAKER_01]: problem. So if you have a certified glider, the glider in this way is safe. But still, a hang glider, you can tamper every hang glider if you

23:24.040 --> 23:32.749
